course: dictionary
lesson: 6478
title: Naughtiness, and what it is not
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Naughtiness is bad behaviour, mostly by children, such as not doing what you are told or being cheeky. It is mild. It is not something seriously wrong or dangerous.
explain: «ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not naughtiness, and it can make sitting still hard.» The word not tells you the two are different. A child who finds sitting still hard is not being naughty. Context clues help you see that.
explain: British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand English use naughtiness. Americans more often say misbehaviour or being bad. Common phrases are "a bit of naughtiness" and "no more naughtiness".
fact: Naughtiness means bad behaviour, especially by children, such as not doing what you are told or being cheeky.
fact: Naughtiness is an uncountable noun, so say "a bit of naughtiness", not "three naughtinesses".
fact: «ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not naughtiness.»
fact: Mild bad behaviour is naughtiness. Something serious or dangerous is not naughtiness.
fact: In American English, people more often say misbehaviour or being bad.
exercise: «Mum said, "No more naughtiness, please!"» What does naughtiness mean here? | Going to bed early | *Bad behaviour, like not listening | A new game | Being very happy
exercise: «ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not naughtiness.» According to the sentence, what is ADHD not? | Something that can make sitting still hard | A difference in how the brain works | *Naughtiness
exercise: What is naughtiness? | Being very tired after a long day | *Bad behaviour, such as not doing what you are told | A kind of sweet | A type of weather
test: Why is a child who finds sitting still hard not being naughty, according to the context? | *Because ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not naughtiness | Because they are very happy | Because they are being cheeky on purpose | Because they are very tired from the walk
test: Which phrase correctly uses naughtiness? | three naughtinesses | *a bit of naughtiness | a naughtiness | naughtinesses everywhere
exam: Which word would an American more often use instead of naughtiness? | Brightness | *Misbehaviour | Cheekiness | Tiredness
